def add(i,title,claim,proof,deps):
 x=copy.deepcopy(t);x.update(id=ids[i],kind='lemma',title=title,statement='Assume AC and DC as inherited from the supplied scheme and cohomology results. '+claim+' Complete proof strategy: '+proof,deps=list(dict.fromkeys(common+deps)),sources={'references':srcs},local_addition=True);p[0]['items'].append(x);by[x['id']]=x
add(0,'Rigidification and effective descent of line bundles',r'''For an abelian variety A/k with identity e, the rigidified line-bundle functor is an fppf sheaf on all k-schemes. Rigidified bundles have no nontrivial automorphisms; normalization L↦L⊗p_T^*e_T^*L^{-1} identifies its classes with Pic(A_T)/Pic(T).''',construction.split('Fix a very ample')[0],['def-rigidified-relative-picard-functor-and-dual-abelian-variety','lem-abelian-scheme-universal-structure-sheaf-sections','lem-faithfully-flat-effective-descent-of-modules-and-algebras','lem-nonaffine-global-sections-flat-field-base-change'])
add(1,'Divisor charts and the separated rigidified Picard diagonal',r'''For projective geometrically integral A/k, sufficiently positive relative effective Cartier divisors with fixed Hilbert polynomial form a finite-type open Hilbert chart D. Its class map to the rigidified Picard sheaf is relatively a smooth projective-space bundle over the appropriate open positive-class subfunctor, on arbitrary tests. The diagonal of that sheaf is represented and separated; after representability the Picard scheme is separated.''',construction.split('Fix a very ample')[1].split('The map D^+')[0]+' The map D^+'+construction.split('The map D^+')[1].split('The image V')[0]+' '+construction.split('Separatedness can')[1].join(['Separatedness can','']),[ids[0],'thm-hilbert-scheme-represents-projective-flat-families','lem-hilbert-noetherian-base-fixed-polarization','lem-proper-flat-fp-cohomology-perfect-complex','lem-cohomology-base-change-finite-free-criterion','lem-projective-coherent-cohomology-finite-and-vanishing','thm-nakayama-lemma']+reg)
add(2,'Picard representation by generic quotient and translates',r'''Over an algebraically closed field, the full rigidified relative Picard functor of an abelian variety is represented by a separated locally finite-type group scheme, with a universal rigidified line bundle. Representation is on every test scheme, including nonreduced tests.''',construction.split('The image V')[1].split('Separatedness can')[0].join(['The image V',''])+' Apply the published generic quotient to the flat finite-type represented linear-equivalence relation from the preceding divisor chart, obtaining its saturated quotientable open W. The quotient Y is exactly the open subfunctor V, by descent through the relatively projective-space covering. Gluing all translated open subfunctors gives the whole representing sheaf, rather than a geometric-point bijection.',[ids[0],ids[1],'thm-nonaffine-generic-scheme-quotient-flat-equivalence-relation','lem-nonaffine-fppf-descent-of-scheme-morphisms','thm-gluing-affine-schemes','cor-weak-nullstellensatz-algebraically-closed-coordinate-form'])
amplekernel=r'''For ample L, K=ker(phi_L) is represented by the just constructed Picard scheme. The normalized family Lambda(L) is trivial on A×K by the full kernel universal property. Over an algebraic closure Y=K^0_red is a smooth connected proper subgroup, hence an abelian subvariety: reduced finite-type groups over a perfect field are smooth by translation of the nonempty smooth locus. Restrict Lambda(L) to Y×Y and pull it back by (id,-id). This makes L|Y⊗[-1]^*L|Y trivial. It is ample, since L|Y is ample, inverse is an automorphism and tensor products of ample sheaves are ample. A trivial ample line bundle on a proper integral variety forces dimension zero: a high power embeds it, but all sections of the trivial bundle are scalars, so the embedding is constant. Thus dimY=0, K is zero-dimensional proper finite type, and therefore finite, including its nonreduced structure. This is EGM2.17–2.19, whose proof was read; no pre-existing ample-kernel theorem is presumed.'''
add(3,'Coherent Kunneth, the tangent bound and the proper-image dual',r'''For an abelian variety A of dimension g over an algebraically closed field, H^1(A,O_A) has dimension at most g, the Picard tangent space at zero is H^1(A,O_A), and the Picard zero component is a smooth proper connected group B of dimension g. Every ample L gives an isogeny phi_L:A→B with finite kernel.''',dual.split('The universal rigidified bundle')[0]+amplekernel+' Invariant differentials trivialize Omega_A: universal translation carries the cotangent space at e to every point, and the inverse translation gives the inverse bundle map. Thus wedge^g Omega_A is trivial. In the proper-image argument local dimension equals tangent dimension g at every translated point, giving smoothness over the algebraically closed field. The image is both open and closed, hence exactly the connected zero component.',[ids[2],'lem-theorem-of-the-square-and-mumford-homomorphism','lem-nonaffine-theorem-of-the-cube-for-abelian-variety','thm-differentials-smooth-locally-free','lem-nonaffine-connected-group-geometrically-connected','thm-nonempty-regular-locus-reduced-variety-perfect-field','thm-regular-equals-smooth-over-perfect-field']+coh+ample)
add(4,'Finite-field descent of the dual and the Poincare bundle',r'''For every field k, the algebraically trivial rigidified Picard subfunctor of A/k is represented by an abelian variety A^vee of dimension dimA and a normalized Poincare bundle P on A×A^vee. The formation and full universal property are compatible with field extension.''',dual.split('The universal rigidified bundle')[1].join(['The universal rigidified bundle','']),[ids[0],ids[3],'lem-nonaffine-finite-field-descent-scheme-with-affine-orbits','lem-nonaffine-fppf-descent-of-scheme-morphisms','lem-faithfully-flat-effective-descent-of-modules-and-algebras','lem-filtered-colimit-fp-scheme-stage','lem-filtered-colimit-fp-sheaf-stage','lem-projective-coherent-cohomology-finite-and-vanishing']+ample)
add(5,'Homogeneous bundles and Mumford surjectivity',r'''For every abelian variety A/k a line bundle M has phi_M=0 exactly when its class belongs to Pic^0; if M is nontrivial and homogeneous then every H^i(A,M) vanishes. Over an algebraic closure every homogeneous M is t_x^*L⊗L^{-1} for some x, for any fixed ample L. The equality ker(phi)=Pic^0 holds as a sheaf on all tests.''',homogeneous.split('For an isogeny')[0]+''' For the all-test kernel identification apply proper-factor rigidity to the morphism A×B→A^vee induced by the universal Poincare bundle: at the zero parameter it is zero, so it is identically zero. On every translate of B the universal Mumford morphism is therefore constant. A nonzero constant morphism cannot become zero after a nonempty scalar extension. This establishes the kernel subfunctor scheme-theoretically, including infinitesimal tests.''',[ids[3],ids[4],'lem-nonaffine-rigidity-proper-geometrically-integral-factor','thm-serre-duality-smooth-projective-variety-locally-free-sheaves']+coh)
cartier=r'''Let H be a finite commutative group scheme over a field k, B=O(H). The finite-dimensional vector-space dual B*=Hom_k(B,k), with multiplication dual to Delta_B and comultiplication dual to multiplication_B, is a commutative Hopf algebra. Its R-valued points are the group-like elements of B⊗R: the transpose of an algebra map B*→R is an element chi with Delta chi=chi⊗chi and counit1, exactly an all-test character H_R→G_m,R; its inverse is supplied by the antipode. Thus Spec B* represents H^D. Double vector-space duality is naturally Hopf-compatible, so H≅H^{DD}, with the evaluation pairing and rank equality. Finite commutative group schemes form an abelian category: the published normal-group quotient represents quotient by kernel; finite-type zero-dimensional separated schemes are finite (split into their finitely many local Artin charts), so quotient and image remain finite. The induced monomorphism from quotient to target is finite and hence closed; the target/image quotient supplies the cokernel. Their fppf sheaf kernel-image identities prove exactness. Cartier duality is an additive contravariant equivalence, therefore preserves exact sequences in reverse. To prove H killed by d=rankH, take an arbitrary character chi over R and h∈H(R). Translation conjugates multiplication by chi on the free rank-d algebra B⊗R, so Norm(t_h chi)=Norm(chi). But t_h chi=chi(h)chi; invertibility of Norm(chi) implies chi(h)^d=1. Apply this to the universal character on H^D and the bidual identification to get [d]H=0. This argument handles nonreduced schemes and characteristic dividing d.'''
add(6,'Finite Cartier duality, exactness and exponent',r'''Finite commutative group schemes have a rank-preserving exact Cartier duality H↦H^D representing all-test Hom(H,G_m), with natural evaluation H≅H^{DD}; a rank-d finite commutative H is killed by d.''',cartier,['thm-nonaffine-group-scheme-normal-subgroup-quotient','lem-nonaffine-group-monomorphism-closed-immersion','thm-structure-theorem-for-artinian-rings','thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases','thm-adjugate-identity-over-a-commutative-ring'])
add(7,'Dual isogeny kernels and canonical biduality',r'''Every isogeny f:A→C has dual isogeny f^vee:C^vee→A^vee whose kernel is (kerf)^D as a group scheme and whose degree equals degf. The canonical Poincare morphism kappa:A→A^{vee vee} is an isomorphism; dual pullback is contravariantly functorial.''',homogeneous.split('For an isogeny')[1].join(['For an isogeny','']),[ids[4],ids[5],ids[6],'thm-nonaffine-group-scheme-normal-subgroup-quotient','lem-faithfully-flat-effective-descent-of-modules-and-algebras','thm-nonaffine-abelian-multiplication-finite-faithfully-flat'])
add(8,'Theta extensions, splitting and isotropic descent',r'''For a line bundle N on A and a finite subgroup H⊂K(N), the theta group over H is a G_m-extension with an alternating bilinear commutator. If the commutator is trivial on H over an algebraically closed field, the extension splits, giving H-linearization and descent of N through A→A/H.''',sym.split('To obtain finite')[0]+''' The theta extension is represented by the invertible scalar-isomorphism torsor of the normalized trivial family on A×K(N). Compose the translation isomorphisms to obtain multiplication; central scalar automorphisms give the G_m kernel. The commutator is central, alternating and bilinear by the group commutator identities. Scheme-theoretic isotropy, the finite Cartier-dual splitting argument and module descent apply to all tests, not only geometric points.''',[ids[0],ids[5],ids[6],'thm-nonaffine-group-scheme-normal-subgroup-quotient','lem-faithfully-flat-effective-descent-of-modules-and-algebras','thm-nonaffine-abelian-multiplication-finite-faithfully-flat'])
vanishing=r'''To represent the locus where the universal defect A×S→A^vee equals zero, pull back the finitely presented ideal of the unit. On a finite-type affine chart of S choose a sufficiently large projective twist of this coherent ideal and finitely many global generators, producing O_A(-n)^m→I. This presentation remains surjective after every test-base change. Its composite to O_A is a list of sections of O_A(n); their coefficient vectors lie in the finite locally free constant-family module H^0(A,O_A(n))⊗O_S. Vanishing of those finitely many coefficients represents the locus on all tests. These closed loci glue independently of the chosen twist. The equality locus is a torsor under B=ker(phi) from the preceding homogeneous-bundle result, so it becomes B after an algebraically closed realization and is smooth over k. The published finite-separable-point theorem then supplies a realization over a finite separable extension.'''
add(9,'Symmetric homomorphisms are Mumford maps',r'''For any field k and any symmetric lambda:A→A^vee, there is a finite separable extension k'/k and a line bundle L on A_{k'} with lambda_{k'}=phi_L. In particular this holds over every separably closed field, including characteristic two.''',sym.split('To obtain finite')[0]+' '+vanishing,[ids[7],ids[8],'lem-nonempty-smooth-scheme-finite-separable-point','lem-projective-coherent-cohomology-finite-and-vanishing','lem-eventual-global-generation-coherent-twists','thm-cohomology-and-base-change'])
add(10,'Polarizations and ampleness under Picard twists',r'''Ample L gives a symmetric isogeny phi_L and every abelian variety admits a polarization. Tensoring an ample L by a homogeneous bundle preserves ampleness. For symmetric lambda=phi_L, the diagonal Poincare pullback is ample exactly when L is ample.''',sym.split('For ample L')[1].join(['For ample L','']),[ids[5],ids[7],ids[9],'def-polarization-of-an-abelian-variety']+ample)
add(11,'Poincare cohomology at the identity',r'''For the normalized Poincare bundle P on A×A^vee, with dimA=g, Rp_{2*}P is concentrated in degree g, where R^gp_{2*}P is the length-one skyscraper at zero. In particular the conclusion retains infinitesimal scheme lengths.''',square.split('For any nondegenerate')[0],[ids[4],ids[5],'thm-serre-duality-smooth-projective-variety-locally-free-sheaves','thm-regular-sequences-give-acyclic-koszul-complexes','thm-regular-local-rings-are-domains-and-cohen-macaulay','thm-projective-resolutions-exist','thm-completion-as-extension-of-scalars','thm-nakayama-lemma']+coh)
add(12,'The square degree of a Mumford map',r'''For a nondegenerate line bundle L on an abelian variety over any field, deg(phi_L)=chi(A,L)^2, including characteristic-dividing degrees and nonreduced kernels. Consequently every polarization has perfect-square degree.''',square.split('For any nondegenerate')[1].join(['For any nondegenerate',''])+' In the Euler-characteristic comparison the precise twist is Lambda(L)⊗p_2^*L=m^*L⊗p_1^*L^{-1}; twisting finite-support direct images by this line bundle preserves their lengths. The automorphism (m,p_1) then identifies this bundle with L external-tensor L^{-1}.',[ids[7],ids[11],'lem-flat-base-change-cohomology','lem-euler-characteristic-additive-short-exact','thm-serre-duality-smooth-projective-variety-locally-free-sheaves']+coh)
x=by['thm-abelian-variety-dual-and-polarization'];x['deps']=list(dict.fromkeys(x['deps']+[ids[4],ids[7],ids[9],ids[10],ids[12]]));x['statement']+=' Complete locally supplied proof route: divisor Hilbert charts and the published generic flat-relation quotient construct representable open Picard subfunctors; translations glue the full all-test Picard functor. A primitive coherent H1 tangent bound plus the proper ample Mumford image constructs Pic0, and finite-field effective descent supplies the arbitrary-field dual and Poincare bundle. Character descent proves dual Cartier kernels and degree cancellation proves biduality. The finite theta extension splits by finite Cartier exactness, including characteristic two, giving symmetric-map realization over a finite separable extension. Poincare cohomology is proved using the nonnegative finite free complex and a Cech/Koszul finite-length argument; Euler characteristic gives degree chi(L)^2. Every source result consumed here is either an exact published interface or a local helper with the argument supplied; no Picard representability or Neron theorem is assumed by citation.'
